                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "b47a0325-1bac-42da-b7f1-cdb0e6c7dde2",
                        "entry_id": "b7a85a2a-23fc-3dba-b76b-c888d90370e2",
                        "tags": [],
                        "form": "ostrakon"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5da79c32-d7cc-31cb-9d98-e4c5af2c9924",
                        "entry_id": "b7a85a2a-23fc-3dba-b76b-c888d90370e2",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Tesson de poterie réutilisé dans l’Antiquité comme support d’écriture ou de dessin, notamment pour servir de bulletin de vote."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Ancient"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f48398c4-187d-4a12-a62b-35d26c846dd0",
                                "sense_id": "5da79c32-d7cc-31cb-9d98-e4c5af2c9924",
                                "text": "Le village dégringolait jusqu’au sud, accroché à la route allant vers le Sahara — jamais parvenu jusqu’à nous sauf par le ciel — et retrouvait cette même route qui arrivait du nord, là où la ville et la mer s’usaient l’une l’autre avec des pierres et du sel. Un vrai ostracon vierge que ce village percé d’un minaret qui se gavait sous mes déambulations de jeunesse."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "aed51e64-686a-4fd4-861d-f890045817dd",
                                "sense_id": "5da79c32-d7cc-31cb-9d98-e4c5af2c9924",
                                "text": "Des ostracons (tessons de terre cuite) et des fragments de papyrus ont également été découverts sur place."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "2911f4a6-8b34-4e21-948d-13f4ecabd8bf",
                        "source_entry_id": "b7a85a2a-23fc-3dba-b76b-c888d90370e2",
                        "source_sense_id": "5da79c32-d7cc-31cb-9d98-e4c5af2c9924",
                        "source_sense_description": "Antiquité",
                        "target_lang_word": "Ostrakon",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Ostrakon",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "caf264ef-fbbd-336c-8095-26bd8db4a7b1",
                                        "entry_id": "c068b089-cdb4-3261-884c-8a93c086cd13",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Scherbe eines Tongefäßes, auf der kurze Notizen gemacht wurden"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "d8d29810-63fe-4172-8321-6caa36887a8d",
                                                "sense_id": "caf264ef-fbbd-336c-8095-26bd8db4a7b1",
                                                "text": "„Bis auf das demotische Ostrakon aus Leiden und das Berliner Ostrakon, die beide der römischen Zeit zuzuordnen sind, entstammen sämtliche Stücke dem Neuen Reich (18.–20. Dynastie, 1550-1070 v.Chr.).“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "ef76669b-0b4f-4bf9-83c4-e04d183a68ce",
                        "source_entry_id": "b7a85a2a-23fc-3dba-b76b-c888d90370e2",
                        "source_sense_id": "5da79c32-d7cc-31cb-9d98-e4c5af2c9924",
                        "source_sense_description": "Antiquité",
                        "target_lang_word": "ostracon",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "ostracon",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9a6c7b77-4833-3b20-a0bf-9fd516dcad99",
                                        "entry_id": "b16847de-244e-3550-971a-29b854c8a093",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A piece of pottery or stone, usually broken off from a vase or other earthenware vessel, especially one used to cast a vote during the Ancient Greek process of ostracism."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2d05d752-15ef-4d84-b0ed-0d40ca28333b",
                                                "sense_id": "9a6c7b77-4833-3b20-a0bf-9fd516dcad99",
                                                "text": "The process in this condemnation was thus : the people being assembled, every man took a tile called ostrakon and carried it to a certain part of the market place, surrounded by wooden rails for that purpose, in which were ten gates for the ten tribes to enter distinctly : in this place the tiles were deposited by each person, and numbered in gross by the archons."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "18904f22-ac56-4b9e-a8af-a2967036242d",
                                                "sense_id": "9a6c7b77-4833-3b20-a0bf-9fd516dcad99",
                                                "text": "Also on view are ostraca, pottery fragments on which Athenians inscribed the names of persons they felt too powerful for the good of the city and deserving of ostracism, or 10 years’ exile, a procedure formalized by Cleisthenes."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e3f6ec95-c85a-4217-bc7a-c27fcbd0302e",
                                                "sense_id": "9a6c7b77-4833-3b20-a0bf-9fd516dcad99",
                                                "text": "If there was sufficient interest, the Athenians could vote once per year to ostracize someone, exiling him from Athens for a period of 10 years. To vote, Athenians scratched the name of the person to be ostracized on potsherds, broken pottery pieces called ostraka—hence the name of the procedure."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
